      {
        "id": 97462,
        "name": "Medic-Tank",
        "username": "Medic-Tank",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/medic-tank/{size}/2107_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-04-02T23:40:39.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Matchstickman\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/matchstickman/48/1880_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Matchstickman:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Is it not possible that working out might increase super strength by adding your natural strength to it?If not I want to see a fat, lazy future Legacy (think humans from Wall-E) who can arm wrestle all comers to a standstill, perhaps in Galactic Strike Force!</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>Well if I take your statement litteraly let see legacy is a pretty tall very cut guy so let's say his natural strenght allows a bench press of a 100 Kg and well his daughter could press between 25 and 50 Kg would it really matter much if you add it on say spider man level superstrenght of lifting 20 tons? Not to say there can't be a difference but if the difference is around 1% of the total it usually doesn't really matter. (I did not take superman strenght as a reference because planet moving strenght is simply ridiculous)</p>",
        "post_number": 71,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:24:50.699Z",
        "reply_count": 0,
        "reply_to_post_number": null,
