      {
        "id": 97463,
        "name": "Ironic",
        "username": "Ironic",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/ironic/{size}/2171_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-04-02T23:57:20.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Sure, sure.</p><p>But the question here (which Christopher has already answered by saying both current Parsons...es have the same raw physical strength) is whether Parsons Superstrength is added or multiplied. &nbsp;If the nature of superstrength were something like \"normal strength times 1000,\" then a difference in raw musclepower so small as to be almost undetectable among normal humans would be almost impossible to overcome between Legacies.</p><p>... of course, all of&nbsp;<em>that</em> further spins out of an issue for how muscle is depected in visual media in general - which is to say, inconsistently at best, especially between men and women. &nbsp;At the end of the day, it's probably sanest for everyone involved to just say \"they're both Legacies and strength hasn't increased between them, so they're equally strong.\"</p><p>You know what I do want to know, though? &nbsp;What would people in the universe of Sentinels comics call \"heroes who inherited their names from other heroes\" (the Bunkers are the main non-Parsons example I know of, but there could be others). &nbsp;Surely, calling such a person a \"legacy\" would just get confusing, yes?</p>",
        "post_number": 72,
        "post_type": 1,
